    is the live grammar rule) never had to be resolved. Left in place because a
    negative exhibit is evidence and because it is the control for what follows.

    ⚠⚠ WHAT THE DISCRIMINATOR ACTUALLY IS: **setParse**. Rows 3 and 4 above are
    the same rule and the same body, differing by ONE CALL:

        genParseTest WITH    setParse   ->  NamE HANGS (137, capped 90s)
        genParseTest WITHOUT setParse   ->  NamE completes, exit 0

    And it is not NamE. `incant/walkPhase` does NOT call setParse, and its sweep
    reports 56 clean refusals; add setParse to its generator and the SAME sweep
    HANGS ON ITS FIRST ITEM (`StatemenT`) with zero refusals reported.

    ⚠ THIS RETRACTS THE PHASE STORY. F-23 read the difference as one-phase versus
    two-phase -- NamE hanging when the grammar is only partly generated. The
    two-phase arm differed by NOT CALLING setParse, and the phase split got the
    credit. Structural claims here hold and causal ones fail; this was a causal
    one.

    ⚠ AND IT WAS PREDICTED IN WRITING BEFORE IT WAS MEASURED, which is why the
    reading is worth something: F-17a says the moment genParseTest calls setParse
    "parse() starts dispatching that rule through defStuff.parseMethod instead of
    the arm chain -- activation happening during generation", and F-18's ruling
    records that `field.parse(...)` "trades crash for INFINITE RECURSION through
    the parseMethod fork". A hang is exactly what that predicts. NOT claimed as
    measured: the mechanism itself. That is the walk.

    The `spOn` field is the toggle -- set 0 to suppress setParse. It is read in
    genParseTest and nowhere else.

    THE FIVE STATIONS. Each is an inert assignment to a UNIQUELY named field,
    so a name-conditioned breakpoint lands on one station only, and each is
    paired with a labelled cerr so the transcript names the station with no
    debugger at all. Nothing downstream reads any of them. All plain `=`,
    never `:=` (fixIts F-22).

        anchorHandDefine      before the hand body is defined (fires at file
                              parse time, inside aCTionDefinE)
        anchorHandCompile     before the hand subject is compiled
        anchorWalkInstall     before the walk generates and installs onto Token
        anchorWalkedCompile   before the walked subject is compiled
        anchorNamECompile     before runNamE's compile, the hang

    ⚠⚠ START HERE -- B9 AND B10 ARE THE TWO THAT MATTER NOW, and the pair is a
    candidate mechanism visible in ONE LOOK rather than a theory:

    B9  GroupRules.mm:12200   ruleStuff->parseMethod = ::parseRule;
        WHERE setParse ARMS THE RULE. Any rule carrying a groupList lands here,
        which is every walked grammar rule. Guarded by `if (!parseMethod)` at
        :12184, so it is ONE-SHOT per rule -- if it does not fire, the rule was
        already armed by an earlier pass. Inspect field->groupBody->tag, and
        that parseMethod was null on entry.

    B10 GroupRules.mm:9945    parseRule entry
        THE FRAME TO WATCH REPEAT. parseRule reads the rule's own CodE at :9949.
        So once B9 has armed a rule, compiling that rule's body re-enters the
        rule through its own generated parse method. ⚠ INSPECT field->tag ON
        EACH HIT: the SAME TAG REPEATING IS THE LOOP, and that is the thing to
        look at when you interrupt the hang -- there is no crash frame to catch.
        This is F-18's ruling arriving from the other side; its text already says
        `field.parse(...)` "trades crash for INFINITE RECURSION through the
        parseMethod fork". Confirming or killing that is the walk.

    THE REST, for the refusal (setParse-absent) side rather than the hang.
    C++ BREAKPOINTS, file:line as of this commit. ⚠ GroupRules.mm is GENERATED,
    so any retok moves these -- each row carries the grep that re-finds it.

    B1  GroupRules.mm:11111   code = field->get("CodE");
        processCode's FIRST read of the subject's body. Inspect `code`: null or
        not, its groupBody ADDRESS, its parent, noPrint, and its text. Run both
        arms and take the first field that differs -- that is the answer.
        re-find:  grep -n 'code = field->get("CodE")' GroupRules.mm   (3 hits,
                  want the one inside processCode, the LAST)

    B2  GroupRules.mm:11112   if (field->isRule) action = code;
        A grammar rule takes `action = code`; a plain field keeps `action =
        field`, and two lines down that becomes ruler->currentMETHOD.
        ⚠ DEMOTED: this was the provenance fork the exhibit was aimed at, and
        the exhibit came back negative -- both arms compile -- so it is no
        longer a lead. Kept because it still explains what currentMETHOD points
        at on each arm.
        re-find:  grep -n 'action = code' GroupRules.mm

    B3  GroupRules.mm:11119   if (result = blockRULE->parse(0))
        THE TEST THAT ACTUALLY REFUSES -- not the message. Step over it and read
        `result`: null IS the refusal. Compare ruler->atRuleMark before and
        after to see how far the body got.
        re-find:  grep -n 'blockRULE->parse(0)' GroupRules.mm

    B4  GroupRules.mm:11125   else ::reportCodeFail(field);
        The backstop. Fires ONLY on refusal, so it is the cheapest "did this arm
        refuse" gate if you want to run first and inspect second.
        re-find:  grep -n 'reportCodeFail(field)' GroupRules.mm

    B5  GroupRules.mm:11328   reportCodeFail entry
        Second backstop, in case B4 is folded. Inspect field->groupBody->tag to
        confirm WHICH rule refused -- this message has named the wrong mechanism
        once before, so trust the pointer over the string.

    B6  GroupRules.mm:2301    if (!isCoded(field->flags.actionType)) goto endCompile;
        compile's first read of the subject. If this jumps on either arm, that
        arm never compiled at all and the exhibit is VOID rather than negative.
        Check it fires-through on both before believing any comparison.

    B7  GroupItem.mm:189      GroupItem::addAttribute
        The install-side write -- the walk's `argument +% codeCopy` lands here.
        ⚠ Inspect grup->parent ON ENTRY: if it is non-null the node is COPIED at
        GroupItem.mm:228, so the node that ends up installed is NOT the node
        that was passed in, and any pointer you captured earlier is stale.

    B8  GroupItem.mm:2035     GroupItem::setContent
        Where `CodE = codeBuffer` lands -- the shared grammar CodE rule taking
        the body text before it is copied onto the subject.

    ⚠ POINTER IDENTITY IS THE WHOLE QUESTION AND INCANT CANNOT ASK IT. Incant
    accessors snapshot by value, which is why showBody exists. Under the
    debugger you get identity for free: compare groupBody ADDRESSES, not tags
    and not text.
    ============================================================================  */
Start();
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search GroupFields Utilities list;

debug Start;
register(Utilities);
define
    codeBuffer buffer;
    openBrace="{";
    closeBrace="}";
    anchorHandDefine=0;
    anchorHandCompile=0;
    anchorWalkInstall=0;
    anchorWalkedCompile=0;
    anchorNamECompile=0;
    spOn=1;
    /*  tokenHandSubject -- TOKEN'S REAL BODY, DEFINED BY HAND THROUGH THE
        DEFINE DOOR. Byte-for-byte the alternation the walk generates for
        `Token`, so the two arms of the exhibit differ in PROVENANCE and not in
        text. Hypothesis 5 of the 2026-08-17 falsification table is the reason
        this rule was chosen: Token's real body was already on record as
        compiling standalone.  */
    anchorHandDefine = 1;
    tokenHandSubject code={
        if QuotE() OR NumbeR() OR StringXP() OR TokenXP() OR Operators();
        return runRuleAction(this);
        };
    /***************************************************************************
        genParseTest walks one rule and generates a code attribute w/parse code for
        the rule.
        Note: getParseTest assumes it just works. But what if it dont?
        Note: the use of actionTypE gate in walkRules to avoid rerunning genParseTest
        if has already been run. The test is at the top of walkRules so you have to
        get into walkRules for the gate to happen.
        Note: we may want to add debugging print messages at top of genParseTest
        and compile, at least on our initial runs on a single rule.
        BTW the no attributes no members case is handled by the first setParse
        call in genParseTest. Look that over with monocle in place and a squinty eye.
    ***************************************************************************/
    /*  ⚠⚠ RETIRED 2026-08-19 -- THIS PREDICTION CAN NO LONGER BE READ, AND THE
        REASON IS IN THIS FILE. It is scored against `CENSUS prune-noPrint`
        counts, and the two census `cerr` lines it counts were REMOVED from
        walkRules in the 2026-08-18 offline edit. The instrument is gone, so no
        run of this file can settle it either way. Kept, not deleted: it is the
        reasoning trail for the mint certification and a provenance that names
        what was predicted before it ran. Do not "repair" it by re-adding the
        counters to make the number appear -- if the question is live again it
        wants a fresh pre-registration against an instrument that exists.

        PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION FOR THE POST-MINT RE-RUN, written down
        BEFORE it ran so it could not be fitted afterwards.
        The 2026-08-17 census measured prune-noPrint at 17, and all seventeen
        were the ONE shared CodE node. After the mint fix gives every rule its
        own CodE -- properly noPrint, because processCode sets that on what it
        attaches -- every member walk that meets a CodE should prune it.
            PREDICTED prune-noPrint = 34
            derived as generated(54) minus empty-condition bodies(20), since a
            rule with no terms never enters the member loop at all.
        READING IT: ~34 confirms per-rule flag hygiene. Still 17 means the mint
        did not change what the walk sees. 54 would mean zero-length rules are
        entering the loop too, which would say `length` is being read after the
        attach rather than before.
        This is the SECOND independent certification of the mint -- bodyT proves
        distinct bodies by POINTER, this proves flag hygiene by CENSUS. Two
        mechanisms, one fix.  */
    genParseTest argument code={
        reset(codeBuffer);
        printTO(codeBuffer);
        print openBrace:;
        if spOn != 0;   setParse(argument);
        if datA != 0;
            if listLengtH; print "rules should not have data and a list; one or the other":;
            cerr "CENSUS leaf-install :" taG:;
            printTO(0);
            return;
        if hasAttributeS;   conjunct = "AND";
        else                conjunct = "OR";
        print `"if ";
        iterate grup on argument;
        while ++grup;
            if spOn != 0;   setParse(grup);
            print $taG "()";
            if nexT;    print _conjunct;
            else        print ";";
        @argument;
        print $"return runRuleAction(this);":;
        print `closeBrace:;
        clear(CodE);
        CodE = codeBuffer;
        codeCopy <- copyOf(CodE);
        codeCopy :. noPrinT;
        argument +% codeCopy;
        argument :. isCodeD;
        printTO(0);
        @CodE;
        print taG "=" CodE:;
        };
    walkRules argument code={
        if actionTypE == 0;
            length  = listLengtH;
            if datA != 0;   print ~$taG "=" argument;
            else            print ~$taG;
            genParseTest(argument);
            compile(argument);
            if length > 0;
                iterate grup on argument;
                while ++grup;
                    if noPrinT;     continue;
                    if binTypE;     continue;
                    if isRule;      walkRules(grup);
        else    cerr taG "already has code so no parse generated":;
        };
    /*  ⚠ THE DRIVER IS AN ACTION, AND ITS `stop()` IS INSIDE IT. That is not
        style -- it is what makes this file finish. Measured 2026-08-19.

        Once the walk installs generated methods, THE LOADER CAN NO LONGER READ
        THE SOURCE THAT FOLLOWS. A top-level `walkRules(X); stop();` therefore
        never reaches its `stop()`: the parser goes back for the next statement,
        finds it cannot read one, and dies with `checkInput: no input source`.
        An action body is parsed ONCE, at define time, into a cached BlocK, so
        everything in here is already-parsed code and needs no loader. Calling
        `stop()` from in here ends the process before the loader is ever asked
        for another statement.

        ⚠ AND THE OLD SHAPE'S EXIT STATUS WAS AN ACCIDENT OF WHAT FOLLOWED IT.
        With five dead `walkRules` lines sitting below the `stop()`, the run
        exited 0 -- the poisoned loader failed to parse them and abandoned the
        file, which is the documented exit-0-on-parse-failure path. Truncate
        those same lines and the identical run exits 139. NEITHER run reached
        `stop()`, and neither said so. That is why the sentinel below is on
        `cerr`: stdout is block-buffered and a crash loses it, so a stdout
        sentinel cannot report the crash it exists to detect.

        MEASURED TARGET MATRIX, this shape, 2026-08-19 binary -- all five reach
        the sentinel, print `stop:`, and exit 0:
            TokenXP 59 lines · Braced 59 · NumbeR 19 · QuotE 7 · StringXP 7

        ⚠ `Start` CHANGED TODAY AND IS NO LONGER AN EXIT-1. F-17e is fixed --
        `compile` now reports through processCode's channel and RETURNS NULL
        instead of exiting -- so a `runParse(Start)` reports many refusals
        instead of dying at the first. Measured after the fix: SIX refusals
        reported (`define` `DEFINing` `;` `DO` `do` `while`) and then the run
        HANGS. Killed at a 120s cap. That hang is the live blocker on a
        one-phase full-grammar walk, and it is what `runNamE` below is for.

        FOR A FULL-POPULATION COUNT, DO NOT USE THIS FILE -- USE
        `incant/walkPhase`. It separates generation from compilation, so it
        reports EVERY refusal instead of stopping at whatever the walk was
        standing on. Measured 2026-08-19, exit 0, sentinel: entered 139,
        generated 56, leaf 60, refused 23, and then 56 compiled 0 / rejected 56.

        ONE TARGET PER RUN. The `stop()` ends the process, and a second walk
        could not read its own driver line anyway. Swap the name below; the
        others are parked here rather than left under the `stop()` as lines
        that look live:
            runParse(QuotE);  runParse(NumbeR);  runParse(StringXP);
            runParse(TokenXP);  runParse(Braced);                            */
    runParse argument code={
        walkRules(argument);
        cerr "PARSER SENTINEL -- walk complete":;
        stop();
        };
    /*  runNamE -- THE NamE HANG, MINIMAL. Built for Tony's Xcode walk,
        2026-08-19. Swap the driver line at the foot of the file to
        `runNamE(NamE);` and the process hangs; break in and look at what is
        CYCLING rather than hunting a crash frame.

        WHY THIS SHAPE AND NOT A WALK. NamE was measured in five configurations
        and it hangs in every ONE-PHASE one, so no prefix is needed and the
        smallest reproducer is the honest one:

            standalone, no prefix, driver frame        137 hang
            standalone, no prefix, walk's call path    137 hang
            after an 18-rule prefix, either frame      137 hang
            after the FULL 30-rule TokenXP prefix      137 hang
            inside `incant/walkPhase`'s TWO-PHASE run  REFUSES CLEANLY

        ⚠ THE LAST ROW IS THE DISCRIMINATOR AND IT IS THE INTERESTING ONE.
        Generate-then-compile over the whole population turns the hang into an
        ordinary `ERROR processCode: NamE parse failed`, and the run finishes.
        Interleaved generate-and-compile hangs. So it is not NamE alone -- it is
        NamE compiled against a grammar that is only PARTLY generated.

        The `compile` call is BARE on purpose. Capturing a command's return with
        `:=` segfaults (fixIts F-22), and that defect faked a whole crash matrix
        before it was found. Nothing in this file captures one.  */
    /*  THE EXHIBIT PAIR. Same body text, two provenances, one verdict each.
        Both bare-compile; neither captures a return (F-22).  */
    runTokenHand argument code={
        cerr "STATION anchorHandCompile -- about to compile the HAND-DEFINED body":;
        anchorHandCompile = 1;
        handOut = compile(argument);
        if handOut;     cerr "HAND    COMPILED -- compile returned a field":;
        else            cerr "HAND    REFUSED  -- compile returned null":;
        cerr "TOKENHAND SENTINEL -- the hand arm ran to completion":;
        stop();
        };
    runTokenWalked argument code={
        cerr "STATION anchorWalkInstall -- about to generate and install onto the rule":;
        anchorWalkInstall = 1;
        genParseTest(argument);
        cerr "STATION anchorWalkedCompile -- about to compile the WALK-INSTALLED body":;
        anchorWalkedCompile = 1;
        walkedOut = compile(argument);
        if walkedOut;   cerr "WALKED  COMPILED -- compile returned a field":;
        else            cerr "WALKED  REFUSED  -- compile returned null":;
        cerr "TOKENWALKED SENTINEL -- the walked arm ran to completion":;
        stop();
        };
    runNamEnoParse argument code={
        cerr "NamE repro, setParse SUPPRESSED -- the control for the hang":;
        spOn = 0;
        genParseTest(argument);
        cerr "STATION anchorNamECompile -- compiling with NO setParse":;
        anchorNamECompile = 1;
        compile(argument);
        cerr "NamEnoParse: compile returned -- NO HANG":;
        cerr "NAMENOPARSE SENTINEL -- the control ran to completion":;
        stop();
        };
    runNamE argument code={
        cerr "NamE repro: generating the body":;
        genParseTest(argument);
        cerr "STATION anchorNamECompile -- about to compile; IT HANGS HERE":;
        anchorNamECompile = 1;
        compile(argument);
        cerr "NamE repro: compile returned -- THE HANG IS GONE, re-measure":;
        stop();
        };
    ;

runParse(TokenXP);
